1
00:00:00,260 --> 00:00:08,640
Wishocracy How to spend twenty-seven point two billion dollars a year on curing disease without it getting stolen by lobbyists,

2
00:00:09,020 --> 00:00:12,400
committees, and people whose main qualification is seniority.

3
00:00:13,160 --> 00:00:16,780
Democracy, but designed by someone who actually wanted it to work.

4
00:00:17,750 --> 00:00:24,840
Congratulations! You've passed a one percent treaty. Now you've got twenty-seven point two billion dollars per year flowing

5
00:00:24,840 --> 00:00:31,300
into the one percent Treaty Fund. That's the good news. The bad news is that humans are involved.

6
00:00:32,080 --> 00:00:39,540
Not all of it is available for allocation. First, the automatic deductions (think of these as the "cost of convincing humans

7
00:00:39,540 --> 00:00:47,360
to stop killing each other" tax): VICTORY Incentive Alignment Bond investors receive ten percent, or two point seven two

8
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:55,120
billion dollars, to repay campaign funders. Ten percent, or two point seven two billion dollars, is allocated to political

9
00:00:55,120 --> 00:01:03,120
incentives to keep politicians aligned. Eighty percent, or twenty-one point eight billion dollars, is available for Clinical

10
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:08,340
Trial Allocation, which is the research share after investor and political allocations.

11
00:01:09,420 --> 00:01:14,660
How do you stop parasitic special interests from stealing that twenty-one point eight billion dollars?

12
00:01:15,260 --> 00:01:23,360
If history is any guide, you don't. Every pool of money humanity has ever created has attracted lobbyists the way a dropped

13
00:01:23,360 --> 00:01:27,700
ice cream cone attracts ants, except ants at least work hard.

14
00:01:28,300 --> 00:01:35,540
Wishocracy is the governance system we use on Wishonia (we named it after ourselves, which your species would call "branding").

15
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:39,960
It replaces committees with code and representatives with mathematics.

16
00:01:40,720 --> 00:01:44,020
We've been running it for four thousand two hundred and ninety-seven years.

17
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:48,420
I'm not proposing an experiment. I'm describing infrastructure.

18
00:01:49,020 --> 00:01:56,360
It lets eight billion people collectively decide how to spend twenty-one point eight billion dollars without turning it

19
00:01:56,360 --> 00:02:00,920
into a bureaucratic nightmare. The difference between this and the N.I.H.

20
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:05,640
is like asking two hundred bureaucrats what you should have for lunch versus asking your stomach.

21
00:02:06,440 --> 00:02:11,080
Your stomach has never once funded a colleague's lunch out of professional courtesy.

22
00:02:11,580 --> 00:02:16,270
How Wishocracy Allocates the Collective-Choice Share of the one percent Treaty Fund.

23
00:02:16,940 --> 00:02:22,580
Your decentralized institutes of health network functions as a decentralized crowdfunding platform.

24
00:02:23,260 --> 00:02:29,960
Anyone can submit a campaign proposal. Wishocracy allocates the part of the medical-research share that requires collective

25
00:02:29,960 --> 00:02:36,120
judgment: infrastructure, common tooling, standards, and other public goods markets do badly.

26
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:41,240
It's Kickstarter, but backed by the G.D.P. of nations instead of your friend's credit card.

27
00:02:42,360 --> 00:02:50,440
What your decentralized F.D.A. handles automatically (no voting required): To determine which treatments get tested, companies

28
00:02:50,440 --> 00:02:54,160
register and patients choose (capitalism doing something useful).

29
00:02:54,900 --> 00:03:01,300
To determine which diseases get researched, patients join trials for their conditions (people tend to be motivated about

30
00:03:01,300 --> 00:03:08,590
their own mortality). Resource allocation within trials is handled by market prices and participant choices.

31
00:03:09,140 --> 00:03:17,100
What Wishocracy Actually Decides: The hard stuff. Allocation across campaign proposals competing for one percent Treaty

32
00:03:17,100 --> 00:03:22,220
Fund funds: Infrastructure Campaigns (the boring but essential plumbing).

33
00:03:22,440 --> 00:03:27,680
Decentralized Framework for Drug Assessment Development. Epic E.H.R.

34
00:03:27,680 --> 00:03:32,900
Integration Project. Security Audit Program. Alternative decentralized F.D.A.

35
00:03:33,300 --> 00:03:39,520
(competition! novel concept for government!). Patient Trial Subsidies (where almost all the money goes).

36
00:03:40,460 --> 00:03:49,360
Patient Trial Subsidies Program (the big one; disability-adjusted life years, or D.A.L.Y.-weighted subsidies that flow automatically

37
00:03:49,360 --> 00:03:54,880
via so every dollar buys the most healthy life-years possible).

38
00:03:55,800 --> 00:04:01,280
Pragmatic Clinical Trial Insurance Pool (liability coverage pooled across all trials).

39
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:06,000
Everything else (data storage, negative results publishing) is a condition of receiving funding, not a separate budget item.

40
00:04:06,900 --> 00:04:12,480
Publishing your results costs nothing. Storing data costs less than nothing, relative to the budget.

41
00:04:13,180 --> 00:04:19,399
These are protocol maintenance, not democratic decisions. You don't vote on whether to pay the electricity bill.

42
00:04:20,839 --> 00:04:28,130
How It Works: Pairwise Comparisons Between Campaigns. Your brain can't rank a list of twenty priorities.

43
00:04:28,400 --> 00:04:33,040
It gives up around item number seven. But it's fantastic at comparing two things.

44
00:04:33,660 --> 00:04:41,540
So instead of asking "Rank these ten thousand campaign proposals," the system asks: "What's more important right now:" Security

45
00:04:41,540 --> 00:04:49,120
audits versus E.H.R. integration? Alternative protocol implementation versus trial insurance pool expansion?

46
00:04:49,860 --> 00:04:54,660
Protocol Provider A versus Protocol Provider B? You make a choice.

47
00:04:55,100 --> 00:05:01,480
You do this a few times with different random pairs. It takes five minutes (less time than you spend choosing a Netflix

48
00:05:01,480 --> 00:05:06,900
show, and considerably more important). Millions of other people do the same.

49
00:05:07,320 --> 00:05:12,440
An algorithm aggregates all these head-to-head comparisons into funding allocations.

50
00:05:13,140 --> 00:05:20,600
No filibustering. No horse-trading. No senators holding medical research hostage because they want a highway in their district.

51
00:05:21,640 --> 00:05:30,500
Your D.F.D.A. allocates resources WITHIN pragmatic clinical trials (patients choose which trials to join, so diseases get

52
00:05:30,500 --> 00:05:38,120
funded in proportion to how many people have them). Wishocracy allocates resources BETWEEN the infrastructure and public

53
00:05:38,120 --> 00:05:44,440
goods campaigns that markets can't handle. One is the engine, the other is the steering wheel.

54
00:05:44,900 --> 00:05:51,560
You need both unless you enjoy driving into walls. Why This Actually Works (Math Warning).

55
00:05:52,360 --> 00:06:00,080
When millions of people make pairwise choices, something almost magical happens (and by "magical" I mean "statistically

56
00:06:00,080 --> 00:06:06,090
inevitable," which is the only kind of magic that actually works): Random pairs prevent gaming.

57
00:06:06,440 --> 00:06:14,140
Corrupting your current system requires buying access to five hundred and thirty-five members of Congress, which is so affordable

58
00:06:14,140 --> 00:06:22,660
that every major industry does it simultaneously. Corrupting Wishocracy would require bribing eight billion random voters,

59
00:06:23,120 --> 00:06:30,400
and the only way to "bribe" eight billion people is to offer them something they actually want, which is the system working

60
00:06:30,400 --> 00:06:36,160
correctly. The attack vector for Wishocracy is giving people what they voted for.

61
00:06:36,800 --> 00:06:44,660
Your security vulnerability is democracy. It's also immune to marketing: there's no ballot measure to link to, no "Vote

62
00:06:44,660 --> 00:06:53,320
Yes on Proposition Seven" ad to run. Each voter sees random pairs, so the only way to advertise is to convince people your

63
00:06:53,320 --> 00:06:57,880
campaign is better than whatever it happens to be compared against, which is just...

64
00:06:58,440 --> 00:07:03,100
making a good argument. Your species finds this deeply unsettling.

65
00:07:04,280 --> 00:07:12,360
Independence is structurally guaranteed. Surowiecki identified four conditions for crowd wisdom: diversity, independence,

66
00:07:13,140 --> 00:07:17,920
decentralization, and aggregation. Your elections destroy the most critical one.

67
00:07:18,900 --> 00:07:26,660
First-past-the-post voting forces strategic voting: people pick the "viable" candidate rather than the best one, so each

68
00:07:26,660 --> 00:07:31,800
ballot reflects what voters think other voters will do rather than what they actually want.

69
00:07:32,480 --> 00:07:40,320
It's an ant death spiral: each ant follows the one ahead, the leader follows the last, and the whole colony walks in a circle

70
00:07:40,320 --> 00:07:47,640
until it dies. Pairwise comparisons can't death-spiral because there's nothing to vote "strategically" about.

71
00:07:48,600 --> 00:07:54,620
"Which matters more: security audits or electronic health record integration, or E.H.R.

72
00:07:54,680 --> 00:08:00,580
integration?" has no spoiler candidate, no wasted vote, no reason to care what your neighbor picked.

73
00:08:01,440 --> 00:08:06,620
You just answer honestly. When millions of people answer honestly, you get wisdom.

74
00:08:07,400 --> 00:08:15,160
When millions of people game a two-option system, you get two candidates that three hundred and thirty million people chose

75
00:08:15,160 --> 00:08:20,500
from three hundred and thirty million people, and nobody likes either of them.

76
00:08:21,440 --> 00:08:28,160
Statistical models (Bradley-Terry, PageRank) extract global preferences from individual comparisons.

77
00:08:28,720 --> 00:08:33,980
The same math that ranks Google search results can rank humanity's medical priorities.

78
00:08:34,580 --> 00:08:38,820
Google uses it to find cat videos. You'll use it to cure cancer.

79
00:08:39,740 --> 00:08:48,000
Progress. Outliers cancel out. The guy who votes "fund my personal jetpack" gets drowned out by the millions voting for

80
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:55,960
"cure my mother's Alzheimer's.". Wisdom of crowds emerges. On Who Wants to Be a Millionaire, the studio audience picked

81
00:08:55,960 --> 00:09:00,900
the right answer ninety-one percent of the time. The "phone a friend" expert?

82
00:09:01,540 --> 00:09:09,700
sixty-five percent. Your species already proved that random people outperform credentialed individuals at picking the right

83
00:09:09,700 --> 00:09:14,100
answer. Wishocracy just applies this to questions that matter.

84
00:09:14,860 --> 00:09:20,560
Example with real numbers: Five million people vote. Each makes twenty comparisons.

85
00:09:21,160 --> 00:09:26,440
That's one hundred million data points. The algorithm processes them in seconds.

86
00:09:26,880 --> 00:09:34,880
The output is: "Allocate ninety-seven percent to patient trial subsidies, one percent to protocol development and maintenance,

87
00:09:35,380 --> 00:09:44,660
one percent to security audits and trial insurance, one percent to electronic health record, or E.H.R., integration...".

88
00:09:44,760 --> 00:09:47,300
No committee meetings were harmed in the making of this budget.

89
00:09:47,880 --> 00:09:55,500
It's democracy without the shouting. The stupidity is still there, but it's evenly distributed across millions of people

90
00:09:55,500 --> 00:10:01,420
and thus cancels out. Your species calls this "the wisdom of crowds."

91
00:10:01,480 --> 00:10:09,480
Mine calls it "obvious." About half of the eight hundred and forty-seven civilizations I've advised adopted some version

92
00:10:09,480 --> 00:10:16,060
of Wishocracy. The other half voted against it, which turned out to be the last thing they voted on.

93
00:10:16,820 --> 00:10:20,880
Your Current System Is Phone-a-Friend (But Your Friend Is Being Bribed).

94
00:10:21,860 --> 00:10:28,100
That ninety-one percent-versus-sixty-five percent comparison is actually generous to your politicians.

95
00:10:28,800 --> 00:10:33,620
Phone-a-friend experts have three advantages over members of Congress: One.

96
00:10:34,080 --> 00:10:40,240
They're trying to get the right answer. A senator on the health appropriations committee is trying to get re-elected.

97
00:10:40,700 --> 00:10:47,320
These are different objectives. One of them occasionally results in good medical research funding by accident.

98
00:10:48,040 --> 00:10:52,940
The other one does it on purpose. Two. They're selected for knowledge.

99
00:10:53,380 --> 00:10:56,330
You phone your friend who knows history for history questions.

100
00:10:57,020 --> 00:11:03,280
Your health appropriations committee chair got there through thirty years of seniority and prolific fundraising.

101
00:11:03,800 --> 00:11:06,900
At no point did anyone check whether they could name a protein.

102
00:11:07,580 --> 00:11:11,420
Three. They're not being paid by one of the answer choices.

103
00:11:11,900 --> 00:11:19,280
If the phone-a-friend expert were receiving one hundred and twenty-seven million dollars from "Answer C," you'd call that

104
00:11:19,280 --> 00:11:26,640
corruption. When a senator receives money from a pharmaceutical company and then votes on pharmaceutical regulation, you

105
00:11:26,640 --> 00:11:33,060
call that "lobbying," which is the same thing but with a syllable that makes it sound boring enough that nobody gets angry.

106
00:11:34,220 --> 00:11:41,460
So your current system isn't sixty-five percent. It's phone-a-friend where your friend doesn't know the subject, isn't trying

107
00:11:41,460 --> 00:11:49,020
to answer your question, and is being paid to say "B." Against that baseline, ninety-one percent seems less like a modest

108
00:11:49,020 --> 00:11:53,160
improvement and more like replacing a broken compass with G.P.S.

109
00:11:54,100 --> 00:12:01,470
The twenty-six-point Gap Is a Floor, Not a Ceiling. Here's the part your economists should find uncomfortable.

110
00:12:02,420 --> 00:12:06,660
Trivia is the BEST CASE for experts. There's one correct answer.

111
00:12:06,720 --> 00:12:13,980
The expert either knows it or doesn't. Nobody is paying them to say "Constantinople" when the answer is "Istanbul."

112
00:12:14,120 --> 00:12:18,760
No lobbyist is taking them to dinner. Their career doesn't depend on getting it wrong.

113
00:12:19,060 --> 00:12:28,540
And they still only hit sixty-five percent. Governance decisions are worse for experts on every dimension: The "right answer"

114
00:12:28,820 --> 00:12:36,060
requires knowledge no individual has. Friedrich Hayek won a Nobel Prize in nineteen seventy-four for explaining why.

115
00:12:36,700 --> 00:12:43,920
No central planner, no matter how brilliant, can aggregate the dispersed knowledge that millions of people carry about their

116
00:12:43,920 --> 00:12:49,400
own diseases, their own priorities, their own willingness to trade one outcome for another.

117
00:12:50,040 --> 00:12:57,000
No committee knows what it's like to have lupus. No appropriations chair knows which rare disease is one breakthrough away

118
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:03,940
from a cure. But the millions of people who have lupus know, and the researchers who are close to a breakthrough know, and

119
00:13:03,940 --> 00:13:07,980
a system that asks all of them knows more than any committee ever could.

120
00:13:08,720 --> 00:13:12,260
Experts have financial incentives to give the wrong answer.

121
00:13:12,860 --> 00:13:18,160
On *Millionaire*, nobody pays the expert to lie. In Congress, it's the business model.

122
00:13:18,860 --> 00:13:24,940
Experts are selected for the wrong criteria. On *Millionaire*, you pick your smartest friend.

123
00:13:24,940 --> 00:13:32,160
In Congress, the selection process optimizes for charisma, name recognition, and the ability to stand at a podium without

124
00:13:32,160 --> 00:13:40,440
saying anything career-ending. The overlap between this skill set and "knows which diseases to fund" is, as far as I can

125
00:13:40,440 --> 00:13:48,360
tell, zero. So if crowds outperform experts by twenty-six percentage points on questions where experts have every possible

126
00:13:48,360 --> 00:13:55,860
advantage, the gap on governance decisions, where experts are compromised, poorly selected, and missing most of the relevant

127
00:13:55,860 --> 00:14:02,680
information, is almost certainly larger. The ninety-one percent-versus-sixty-five percent gap is the floor.

128
00:14:03,060 --> 00:14:11,240
The ceiling is the Soviet Union, which was the most ambitious phone-a-friend experiment in history: a small group of credentialed

129
00:14:11,240 --> 00:14:16,720
experts making resource allocation decisions for two hundred and eighty-six million people.

130
00:14:17,280 --> 00:14:24,920
It ran for sixty-nine years. It did not go well. Why Markets Solved This (and Why You Still Need Wishocracy).

131
00:14:26,020 --> 00:14:32,780
Hayek's solution to the knowledge problem was markets. Prices aggregate dispersed information automatically: if millions

132
00:14:32,780 --> 00:14:36,660
of people want more of something, the price goes up, and producers make more.

133
00:14:36,980 --> 00:14:43,540
No committee required. Your species has been doing this for thousands of years and it works so well that you barely notice

134
00:14:43,540 --> 00:14:48,100
it, the way you barely notice breathing until someone puts a pillow over your face.

135
00:14:48,680 --> 00:14:53,740
But markets have a blind spot. They only work when someone can profit from the solution.

136
00:14:54,420 --> 00:14:59,360
Drug companies will fund research into diseases that sell expensive pills to rich countries forever.

137
00:14:59,820 --> 00:15:07,040
They will not fund cures for rare diseases, off-patent treatments, or anything where the profit model is "everyone benefits

138
00:15:07,040 --> 00:15:14,050
but nobody pays." These are called public goods, and markets handle them the way your cat handles a bath.

139
00:15:14,440 --> 00:15:21,420
Wishocracy is what you build when you need Hayek-style information aggregation but can't rely on price signals.

140
00:15:21,820 --> 00:15:28,640
It takes the same principle (millions of individual decisions aggregated into collective intelligence) and applies it to

141
00:15:28,640 --> 00:15:36,480
the things markets won't touch. It's the free market's missing organ: the part that funds what's important instead of just

142
00:15:36,480 --> 00:15:40,740
what's profitable. Hayek diagnosed the disease in nineteen forty-five.

143
00:15:41,460 --> 00:15:46,520
On Wishonia, we built the cure several millennia earlier. Better late than never.

144
00:15:47,340 --> 00:15:57,500
From Priorities to Projects (Where Wishes Become Tasks). Great, so the Pairwise Slider Allocation tells you "Curing Alzheimer's"

145
00:15:57,560 --> 00:16:03,980
is a top priority. Wonderful. Now what? Having a priority without a plan is just a wish.

146
00:16:04,280 --> 00:16:10,840
And wishes, as a rule, don't cure anything. Wishocracy translates that priority into action.

147
00:16:11,940 --> 00:16:21,080
A.I. Breaks It Down: An A.I. takes the impossible goal of "Cure Alzheimer's" and breaks it into thousands of smaller, concrete,

148
00:16:21,360 --> 00:16:25,360
fundable tasks. "Cure Alzheimer's" becomes "Map protein structures."

149
00:16:26,260 --> 00:16:31,540
That becomes "Run AlphaFold on these sequences." That becomes "Rent computing time."

150
00:16:32,480 --> 00:16:37,360
Every impossible problem is just a series of possible steps arranged in a line.

151
00:16:37,980 --> 00:16:44,020
Your species figured this out for building pyramids four thousand five hundred years ago but keeps forgetting to apply it

152
00:16:44,080 --> 00:16:50,780
to medicine. The Bounty Board: The system posts these tasks to a global marketplace.

153
00:16:51,200 --> 00:16:56,200
It's like eBay, but for saving humanity. WANTED: A cure for Alzheimer's.

154
00:16:56,680 --> 00:17:02,140
BOUNTY: ten billion dollars. WANTED: A map of all protein misfolding patterns.

155
00:17:02,600 --> 00:17:09,760
BOUNTY: five hundred million dollars. The World Competes: The best teams from around the world, from M.I.T.

156
00:17:10,020 --> 00:17:16,540
to some kid in a garage in Lagos, bid on these tasks. The system funds multiple approaches in parallel.

157
00:17:17,040 --> 00:17:21,650
The ones that show promise get more funding. The ones that fail lose funding instantly.

158
00:17:22,300 --> 00:17:29,320
It's venture capital, but for not dying. Your species already uses this method to decide which restaurants survive and which

159
00:17:29,320 --> 00:17:33,160
phone apps get downloaded. Time to try it with the thing that kills you.

160
00:17:33,840 --> 00:17:41,420
Why the Algorithm Is the Constitution. Your species has a habit of writing beautiful rules and then immediately finding

161
00:17:41,420 --> 00:17:47,500
people to break them. Your Constitution guarantees free speech, and your government classifies documents.

162
00:17:47,980 --> 00:17:52,280
Your laws prohibit bribery, and your lobbying industry does it with a receipt.

163
00:17:53,100 --> 00:18:00,680
Every protection you've ever designed relies on humans to enforce it, and humans can be bought, threatened, promoted, or

164
00:18:00,680 --> 00:18:08,240
distracted by a sufficiently interesting scandal. On Wishonia, we tried enforcement-based protections for about two hundred

165
00:18:08,240 --> 00:18:15,480
years before concluding that constitutional constraints enforced by people are just suggestions with better typography.

166
00:18:16,020 --> 00:18:23,630
So we put the protections in the math. Algorithmic Protections (Rules Nobody Can Break Because They're Not Rules).

167
00:18:24,100 --> 00:18:30,800
Rare disease protection. Your instinct is to write a law: "At least X percent of funding must go to rare diseases."

168
00:18:31,280 --> 00:18:37,580
Then someone defines "rare" in a way that excludes their rival's disease, and a committee meets quarterly to argue about

169
00:18:37,580 --> 00:18:40,620
the definition, and a lobbyist takes the committee chair to dinner.

170
00:18:41,560 --> 00:18:49,660
We solved this with three algorithms: One. disability-adjusted life years, or D.A.L.Y.-weighting per capita, not total burden.

171
00:18:50,220 --> 00:18:57,140
A disease that ruins one thousand lives completely gets funded proportionally to one that mildly inconveniences ten million.

172
00:18:57,800 --> 00:19:00,380
The algorithm doesn't know how many people have the disease.

173
00:19:00,720 --> 00:19:07,980
It knows how much each person suffers. This is the difference between "how many votes does this disease have" and "how badly

174
00:19:07,980 --> 00:19:12,380
does this disease need fixing." Your democracy counts the first.

175
00:19:12,400 --> 00:19:20,040
Wishocracy counts the second. Two. Quadratic funding. The number of unique supporters matters more than total dollars.

176
00:19:20,620 --> 00:19:27,960
Ten thousand people each caring deeply about a rare disease outweigh one billionaire caring mildly about a common one.

177
00:19:28,500 --> 00:19:34,780
The math: funding is proportional to the square of the sum of square roots of individual contributions.

178
00:19:35,220 --> 00:19:41,440
If that sentence made your eyes glaze over, good. Glazing-over is what protects it from politicians, who can only corrupt

179
00:19:41,440 --> 00:19:45,300
things they understand, which is a surprisingly small category.

180
00:19:46,480 --> 00:19:54,820
Three. Diminishing returns. Each additional dollar spent on an already well-funded disease buys less marginal health improvement.

181
00:19:55,420 --> 00:20:00,750
The hundredth million for cancer research buys less than the first million for a neglected tropical disease.

182
00:20:01,400 --> 00:20:08,380
The algorithm knows this because it measures D.A.L.Y.s prevented per dollar and maximizes that, not total dollars spent.

183
00:20:08,940 --> 00:20:13,620
A congressman maximizes total dollars spent because that's what the press release measures.

184
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:19,520
The algorithm maximizes actual health because nobody programmed it to care about press releases.

185
00:20:20,300 --> 00:20:28,200
Transparency. There is no "classify" button. The system publishes all allocations, all results, all failures.

186
00:20:28,560 --> 00:20:33,240
Not because a law requires it, but because the publication function doesn't have an off switch.

187
00:20:33,900 --> 00:20:40,540
You can't vote to hide data for the same reason you can't vote to make gravity go sideways: the system doesn't have that

188
00:20:40,540 --> 00:20:45,390
gear. Every failed trial, every dollar spent, every outcome measured.

189
00:20:45,920 --> 00:20:49,400
Your current system publishes roughly half of clinical trial results.

190
00:20:50,040 --> 00:20:54,300
The other half disappear into filing cabinets because the results were embarrassing.

191
00:20:55,240 --> 00:21:00,040
On Wishonia, "embarrassing results" is not a category. "Results" is.

192
00:21:00,600 --> 00:21:08,100
The filing cabinet was never built. Corruption resistance. First, the treaty strips out the investor and political slices

193
00:21:08,560 --> 00:21:15,060
before discretionary allocation begins, so the people with the strongest incentive to game the system do not control the

194
00:21:15,060 --> 00:21:23,200
medical-research share. Within that medical share, patient participation and protocol choice automatically direct the trial-by-trial

195
00:21:23,200 --> 00:21:30,920
money that behaves well under market signals. Wishocracy governs the remainder: the infrastructure, standards, and other

196
00:21:30,920 --> 00:21:37,640
shared goods that actually require collective judgment. If someone corrupts the voting layer, they still do not capture

197
00:21:37,640 --> 00:21:43,580
bond payouts, political incentives, or the patient-directed flow of trial participation money.

198
00:21:43,900 --> 00:21:48,320
They only distort the subset that genuinely depends on collective choice.

199
00:21:48,940 --> 00:21:55,600
Compare that to your current system, where a single senator on the appropriations committee can redirect nearly everything.

200
00:21:56,620 --> 00:22:03,440
Your corruption ceiling is dramatically lower because the architecture narrows what is even available for human discretion.

201
00:22:04,480 --> 00:22:09,700
The Two Hard Rules. Almost everything is algorithmic. Almost.

202
00:22:10,420 --> 00:22:17,600
Two constraints require actual rules because they define what the system *is*, not how it *operates*: One.

203
00:22:17,700 --> 00:22:24,720
Scope. The fund is for health and medical research. This is defined by the one percent treaty, not by wishocracy.

204
00:22:25,460 --> 00:22:32,560
No amount of pairwise voting can redirect it to weapons, infrastructure, or your senator's cousin's construction company.

205
00:22:33,160 --> 00:22:38,680
The system doesn't accept proposals outside scope. It's not that voting "weapons" would fail.

206
00:22:39,140 --> 00:22:42,340
It's that "weapons" isn't a category the ballot can contain.

207
00:22:42,900 --> 00:22:49,900
You can't write in "build a missile" on a medical research ballot for the same reason you can't deposit a sandwich at an

208
00:22:49,900 --> 00:22:55,600
A.T.M. The machine doesn't accept that input. Two. Bodily autonomy.

209
00:22:56,400 --> 00:23:02,230
You modify your own neurochemistry. Nobody else's. Not by majority vote.

210
00:23:02,260 --> 00:23:09,460
Not by executive order. Not by a very convincing TED talk. The system has no "modify this person" function.

211
00:23:09,960 --> 00:23:17,240
But unlike transparency (where the absence of a button is sufficient protection), bodily autonomy requires a legal prohibition

212
00:23:17,240 --> 00:23:21,320
because someone could build coercion mechanisms *outside* the system.

213
00:23:21,760 --> 00:23:28,160
So this one is a law. The only law. On Wishonia, it's the shortest statute on the books: "Self."

214
00:23:28,780 --> 00:23:34,120
Everything else is math. What Wishocracy Measures (The Anti-Fed).

215
00:23:34,880 --> 00:23:42,960
Your Federal Reserve has two targets: "maximum employment" (keep everyone too busy to ask questions) and "two percent inflation"

216
00:23:43,620 --> 00:23:47,060
(steal two percent of purchasing power annually and call it stability).

217
00:23:47,940 --> 00:23:55,020
By these metrics, a medieval serf working sunup to sundown while his currency devalues is a policy success.

218
00:23:55,580 --> 00:24:02,320
The metrics are not broken. They measure exactly what they were designed to measure, which is obedience and extraction.

219
00:24:02,820 --> 00:24:11,240
(For the full horror show, see How Central Banks Fund Your Death.) Wishocracy measures four things: One.

220
00:24:11,420 --> 00:24:18,080
Median health-adjusted life years. Not average (which lets billionaires living to one hundred and twenty mask millions dying

221
00:24:18,080 --> 00:24:23,860
at sixty). Not G.D.P. (which counts the cost of your chemotherapy as economic growth).

222
00:24:24,700 --> 00:24:32,740
*Median*: the person in the middle. Health-adjusted: years lived well, not years spent deteriorating in a hospital that

223
00:24:32,740 --> 00:24:38,520
charges fifty dollars for aspirin. Is the typical person living longer and healthier?

224
00:24:38,740 --> 00:24:43,820
Yes or no. If no, the system is failing. No amount of G.D.P.

225
00:24:43,940 --> 00:24:50,440
growth excuses it. Two. disability-adjusted life years, or D.A.L.Y.s, prevented per dollar.

226
00:24:51,060 --> 00:24:56,380
Efficiency. Is each dollar buying the maximum possible reduction in human suffering?

227
00:24:56,900 --> 00:25:01,080
The algorithm optimizes this automatically via diminishing returns curves.

228
00:25:01,840 --> 00:25:05,540
Your current system doesn't measure this at all. The N.I.H.

229
00:25:05,620 --> 00:25:12,240
measures "dollars spent," which is like a restaurant measuring "ingredients purchased" instead of "customers fed."

230
00:25:12,280 --> 00:25:16,540
You can spend a fortune and accomplish nothing. This metric makes that visible.

231
00:25:17,100 --> 00:25:24,260
Three. Treatment access rate. What percentage of people who need a validated treatment can actually get it?

232
00:25:24,980 --> 00:25:31,180
If you cure cancer but only rich people can afford the cure, the access rate is low and the system is failing.

233
00:25:32,020 --> 00:25:37,940
This metric is why the algorithm pushes toward cheap, scalable treatments over expensive bespoke ones.

234
00:25:38,580 --> 00:25:43,700
Not because someone wrote a rule. Because the algorithm scores better when more people are reached.

235
00:25:44,480 --> 00:25:48,920
Four. Discovery velocity. New treatments validated per year.

236
00:25:49,520 --> 00:25:54,560
Is the system getting faster at turning money into cures? This is the speedometer.

237
00:25:55,180 --> 00:25:59,620
The other three metrics tell you where you are. This one tells you how fast you're moving.

238
00:26:00,600 --> 00:26:04,000
These are the metrics your central banks should have been using for the last century.

239
00:26:05,320 --> 00:26:11,520
They didn't, because "median health-adjusted life years" would have revealed that your economy was making people sicker

240
00:26:11,520 --> 00:26:19,060
while telling them they were richer. It is much easier to target "two percent inflation" and declare victory while your

241
00:26:19,060 --> 00:26:23,860
citizens die of diseases that were curable with the money you printed for banks.

242
00:26:24,660 --> 00:26:29,200
On Wishonia, we don't declare victory. The algorithm updates the dashboard.

243
00:26:29,640 --> 00:26:34,600
Everyone can see the numbers. There is no "hide" button. I believe I mentioned that.
